WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.009
These two men were the first to climb to the
top of Mt. Everest.

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:10.700
Edmund Hillary, from New Zealand, and Tenzing
Norgay, a Sherpa from from Nepal and India,

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.969
became global celebrities after reaching the
summit, where Hillary snapped this photo of

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:22.110
Norgay holding their national flags. Since
then, many have followed in their footsteps,

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:27.450
raising their own flags at the peak. Summit
bids have created a lucrative industry —

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:32.820
and a perilous one; hundreds have died or
been injured during the climb. All in the

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.350
pursuit of one goal: reaching the highest
point on Earth. But the thing is, most of

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:43.600
the people who have stood on top of Mt. Everest
have climbed to slightly different heights—

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:50.370
For Hillary and Norgay, it was 8,840 meters.
For this British Army officer who summited

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:57.610
in 1976, the height was 8,848 meters. This
Sherpa guide and his Swedish client climbed

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:04.730
29,035 feet or 8,850 meters in 1999. And at
the end of 2020, the height of Mount Everest

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:11.050
changed again — it’s now officially 8,849
meters. These changes are small — and probably

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:15.120
don’t really matter to the people who’ve
reached the summit. But the reason why Mount

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.150
Everest’s height keeps changing tells a
story about how we measure mountains—and

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:25.560
about who gets to do the measuring.
We have only one Mt. Everest in the world.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:36.360
But the one mountain had several heights.
This is the Himalayan mountain range, and

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:42.361
here is Mount Everest, with one side in Tibet
and another side in Nepal. In Nepal, the mountain

00:01:42.361 --> 00:01:48.430
is known as Sagarmatha; in Tibet, it’s called
Chomolungma. Everest is a colonial name, named

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:52.500
for this British official, George Everest.
And that’s because India, Nepal’s neighbor,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.390
was under British rule
when Everest was first measured. British and

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:02.910
Indian surveyors started a massive mapping
project in 1802, at one point led by George

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:08.479
Everest. Called the Great Trigonometrical
Survey. They measured as much of India’s

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:12.939
land as they could, using an instrument like
this, called a theodolite.

00:02:12.939 --> 00:02:18.330
It’s the distant ancestor of what land surveyors
and engineers use today—to basically do

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:24.150
the same thing —measure the angles between
two horizontal points, and use basic trigonometry

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:30.310
to measure the location and distance to a
third point. But when surveyors from the Great

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:36.560
Trigonometrical Survey reached the Himalayas
in the 1840s, they ran into a very tall, vertical

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.871
problem. Measuring the height of a mountain
is more complicated than just measuring from

00:02:40.871 --> 00:02:46.999
the ground to the peak. You have to know where
sea level is. Because sea level is relatively

00:02:46.999 --> 00:02:51.090
similar throughout the globe, it’s the base
that most natural heights on earth are measured

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:56.510
from. But there is no sea or ocean immediately
next to the Himalayas. So surveyors in the

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:01.269
mid-1800s had to walk from the Bay of Bengal to translate sea level to

00:03:01.269 --> 00:03:07.370
the Himalayas, which took years. Surveyors
couldn't enter Nepal at the time,

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:12.900
so they did this from over 100 miles
away, across the border in India.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.849
Only then could they measure the distance
between two points at sea level then aim the

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:21.930
theodolite to the peak. That’s how they
measured the Himalayas, 100 years before anyone

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:26.200
reached Everest’s summit. And that’s how,
in 1855, the first official measurement of

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:34.279
Mount Everest was recorded: 8,840 meters.
After that first measurement, scientists from

00:03:34.279 --> 00:03:38.599
around the world began documenting their own
heights. They were never too far off from

00:03:38.599 --> 00:03:44.549
that first one, but fluctuated anywhere from
⅓ of a meter to 72 meters. One reason those

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:48.629
numbers differ is because it’s still really
hard to calculate sea level. The sea might

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:54.300
seem relatively smooth compared to earth’s
erratic topography. But water is uneven too:

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:59.340
tides go up and down — and, thanks in part
to global warming, sea levels are rising.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.959
The global mean sea level is an average of
all these fluctuations. But when surveyors

00:04:03.959 --> 00:04:08.599
want to measure a mountain’s height, they
have to be more precise. That means considering

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:13.180
something called the ellipsoid — the bulge
at Earth’s equator due to the centrifugal

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:19.419
force of its rotation. And areas of the Earth
with more density, like mountain ranges, affect

00:04:19.419 --> 00:04:24.490
gravity and therefore the height of sea level.
Taking variations on gravity into account,

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:30.500
this is Earth’s true sea level, called the
geoid, which is full of dimples and bumps.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.380
When surveyors want to measure Everest, they
have to precisely consider all these conversions,

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:42.120
which explains some of the variations in height.
But there’s another reason the height of

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.130
a mountain might shift, that has to do with
the origin story of the Himalayas.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:50.270
These mountains started forming 50 million
years ago when the Indian continent collided

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:55.620
with the Asian continent. That collision hasn’t
stopped happening, even if we can’t see

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:00.110
it. Geologists think that the Himalayas are
still rising 5 millimeters a year, or a quarter

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.370
of an inch.
The tectonic shifts causing that growth also

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:07.860
cause earthquakes in the region, which can
shift the height of mountains.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:12.160
So when Nepal suffered a devastating earthquake
in 2015,

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.669
Scientists knew Everest’s height had probably
changed.

00:05:14.669 --> 00:05:21.180
Nepalese surveyors decided to investigate.
Being the Everest-lying country all responsibility

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.130
is to clarify the question regarding the height
of Mount Everest.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:31.800
Khim Lal Gautam, climbed to the top of Everest
in 2019 to take a new measurement.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:37.790
And brought with him a tool that’s been
helping surveyors since the 1980s: a GPS receiver.

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:44.020
Gautam lingered at the peak of Everest for
nearly 2 hours in the middle of the night,

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:49.690
which is an eternity anywhere in the oxygen-deprived
altitude above 8,000 meters, known as The

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:54.590
Death Zone. He endured it to receive as many
satellite pings as he could.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:58.870
GPS can accurately measure height through
the time it takes a satellite signal to reach

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:04.189
a receiver. But that signal gives a height
based on Earth’s ellipsoid, not the geoid.

00:06:04.189 --> 00:06:08.139
Which means it still doesn’t solve for the
most important part of mountain surveying:

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:13.520
establishing the local sea level.Doing that
with precision still requires surveying on

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:14.520
land.
We planned for the study of 50 kilometers

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:15.689
at the east and 50 kilometers at the west,
from northern border to the southern border.

00:06:15.689 --> 00:06:22.339
This was our study area and within this region
we had around 300 control points.

00:06:22.339 --> 00:06:27.360
Susheel Dangol led the Nepalese survey from
2017 to 2019.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.080
To find the geoid height, they measured gravity
through an instrument like this.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:36.310
The main motive of this... is to get the mean
sea level.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:40.889
Around the same time that Susheel and Gautum
were surveying, a Chinese team was, too, from

00:06:40.889 --> 00:06:46.139
the Tibetan side of Everest. And in December
2020, they made a joint announcement about

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:56.000
their agreed-upon new measurement: 8,848.86
meters.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:01.860
Since 1855, all official measurements of Everest
from the Nepalese side were done by colonial

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:07.250
or foreign surveyors. Which makes this number
significant: it’s the first time in history

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:12.381
Nepal measured their own mountain. 

00:07:12.381 --> 00:07:15.306
We are very proud to be the people of the Everest country. Mount Everest country, we are the people of
the Sagamartha country. 

00:07:15.383 --> 00:07:21.013
We feel proud to do the Everest measurement ourselves because we have not done that task  ourselves.

00:07:21.013 --> 00:07:26.310
 
In the future, Everest’s height might still inch up or down.  

00:07:27.311 --> 00:07:34.400
but for anyone who reaches
the top, they will still be on the roof of the world.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.111
So we just showed you difficult it is to find mean sea level. 

00:07:37.111 --> 00:07:41.271
But there's actually a couple other ways you can measure the height of a mountain. 

00:07:41.271 --> 00:07:45.906
that's less universally-used but would make some mountains actually taller than Everest.  

00:07:45.906 --> 00:07:51.433
If you take the measurement from Earth's center instead of sea level, Mount Chimborazo in Ecuador 

00:07:51.433 --> 00:07:53.309
would actually be taller than Everest. 

00:07:53.309 --> 00:07:58.691
And that's because it's located closer to the Equator, so the ellipsoid would push it taller.  

00:07:58.691 --> 00:08:01.519
And if you take a simple base to peak measurement.

00:08:01.519 --> 00:08:04.105
Mauna Kea in Hawaii would be taller than Everest 

00:08:04.105 --> 00:08:09.342
and that's because a majority of the mountain is actually 
 underwater, so below sea level. 

00:08:09.342 --> 00:08:11.699
I included a link in the description if you want to read more about that. 

